  "answer_text": "Great question! Here is a comprehensive breakdown of where opioids come from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. In its raw state, opium is a gummy latex extracted from *Papaver somniferum*.\n\nOpioids generally fall into **three categories**:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the **resin of the plant's seed pod**. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**. Natural opioids such as **codeine, heroin, morphine, and opium** can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds, and may be made in modern laboratory and manufacturing facilities. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that manufacturers use to make semi-synthetic opioids. Examples include hydrocodone, oxycodone, and heroin.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are entirely man-made. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids that are derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are **not made from opium poppy products** \u2014 instead, they are made from other precursor chemicals. Synthetic opioid compounds are made to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors. Examples include fentanyl, methadone, and tramadol.\n\n---\n\n### A Brief History\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun about **3000 BCE**, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks. In the 20th century, experiments with opiates led to the development of synthetic compounds that have similar effects on the body. To distinguish them from natural opiates, these synthetic compounds were called **opioids** (meaning \"opiate-like\").\n\n---\n\n### How Do They Work?\nOpioids derive from, or mimic, natural substances found in the opium poppy plant and work in the brain to produce a variety of effects, including **pain relief**. However, opioid drugs, even those prescribed by a doctor, are very addictive and can lead to opioid use disorder. Using any opioids for their euphoric effect, even for a short time, raises the risk of substance use disorder, overdose, and death.",
